**Differential flow:**

Once the reference flow is estimated, the next step is to calculate differential flow (e.g. as a function of \(p_{T}\)) of the particle of interest (POI), which needs another two vectors \(\vec{p}\) and \(\vec{q}\). Particles used to estimate reference flow are called reference particles (REP). For particles labeled as POI:

\[p_{n}\equiv\sum\limits_{i=1}^{m_{p}}e^{in\psi_{i}}. \tag{5.23}\]

For particles labeled as both POI and REP:

\[q_{n}\equiv\sum\limits_{i=1}^{m_{q}}e^{in\psi_{i}}. \tag{5.24}\]

Then the reduced single-event average 2- and 4-particle correlations are:

\[\langle 2^{\prime}\rangle=\frac{p_{n}Q_{n}^{*}-m_{q}}{m_{p}M-m_{q}} \tag{5.25}\]

\[\langle 4^{\prime}\rangle= [p_{n}Q_{n}Q_{n}^{*}Q_{n}^{*}-q_{2n}Q_{n}^{*}Q_{n}^{*}-p_{n}Q_{ n}Q_{2n}^{*} \tag{5.26}\] \[-2\cdot Mp_{n}Q_{n}^{*}-2\cdot m_{q}|Q_{n}|^{2}+7\cdot q_{n}Q_{n}^ {*}\] \[-Q_{n}q_{n}^{*}+q_{2n}Q_{2n}^{*}+2\cdot p_{n}Q_{n}^{*}+2\cdot m_{ q}M\] \[-6\cdot m_{q}]/[(m_{p}M-3m_{q})(M-1)(M-2)],\]

where \(m_{p}\) is the total particles of interest in an event and total number of particles which can be marked as both POI and REP are \(m_{q}\). Now the average over N events can be obtained as follows:

\[\langle\langle 2^{\prime}\rangle\rangle\equiv\frac{\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N} \left(W_{\langle 2^{\prime}\rangle}\right)_{i}\langle 2^{\prime}\rangle_{i}}{ \sum\limits_{i=1}^{N}\left(W_{\langle 2^{\prime}\rangle}\right)_{i}} \tag{5.27}\]

